  {
   "type": "faq",
   "h": "Questions validation and engineering teams ask",
   "items": [
    {
     "q": "As a new supplier, how do you check your design protects our RO through seasonal swings before we order?",
     "a": "We ask for [months of history] of utility and site data, find the worst month for each parameter (temperature, turbidity, organics, chlorine or chloramine dose, hardness) and size every bed and the softener against those months. You see the design basis, month by month, before you place the order."
    },
    {
     "q": "Is pretreatment inside the turnover package and the validation boundary?",
     "a": "It is in the turnover package either way: drawings, material certificates, media and resin certificates, calibration certificates, FAT records. Whether it sits inside the validated boundary is your call in the risk assessment; we design and document it so either answer holds up."
    },
    {
     "q": "How do you control bioburden in carbon beds without chemical carryover to the RO?",
     "a": "Carbon beds are sanitized with [hot water or steam, at the temperature and hold time on this page], backwashed on a schedule and replaced at [carbon replacement interval]. No biocide is dosed ahead of the RO, so there is nothing to carry over."
    },
    {
     "q": "Is pretreatment part of the validated system?",
     "a": "It depends on where your risk assessment draws the boundary. Many sites qualify from the RO inlet onward and treat pretreatment as supporting equipment under good engineering practice. Its performance still decides whether the RO meets its limits, so we document it to the same standard, test it at FAT and SAT, and supply installation and operational qualification (IQ/OQ) protocols where you bring it into scope."
    },
    {
     "q": "Do we have to prove our feed water meets drinking water standards?",
     "a": "The USP and Ph. Eur. monographs make PW from drinking water, and USP <1231> names it as the minimum source quality, so an auditor will expect you to show it. For municipal supply, the utility's reports plus your own periodic testing at the plant inlet are the usual evidence. For a private well you are the utility, so you need a sampling programme against the Guidelines for Canadian Drinking Water Quality or the NPDWR, including microbiological tests. We can propose the parameters and frequency."
    },
    {
     "q": "Our municipality uses chloramine. Is standard carbon enough?",
     "a": "Usually not at a reasonable bed size. Chloramine reacts on carbon far more slowly than free chlorine, and slower still in cold water. We size catalytic carbon or two beds in series on contact time at peak flow and winter temperature, or dose bisulfite, and measure total chlorine at the RO inlet, because a free chlorine analyser does not see chloramine."
    },
    {
     "q": "What happens to the ammonia from chloramine?",
     "a": "Removing chloramine releases ammonia. RO rejects it well as ammonium, but at the higher pH used to control carbon dioxide more of it is dissolved ammonia gas, which passes the membrane and loads the EDI. So the chloramine removal here and the CO₂ strategy on the PW skid are designed together. See Purified Water generation."
    },
    {
     "q": "Why do our carbon bed counts spike?",
     "a": "Carbon removes the disinfectant and adsorbs organics, so it grows biofilm, faster in summer and when flow stops. Counts after the bed that rise between sanitizations usually mean the interval is too long, the bed stagnates at no demand or the media is exhausted. We trend counts before and after the bed and set sanitization from that data."
    },
    {
     "q": "Why measure SDI on site rather than from the utility report?",
     "a": "Utilities report turbidity, not SDI, and SDI depends on what the water picks up between the treatment plant and your tap. ASTM D4189 measures it in 15 minutes at your cartridge filter outlet. We test during the feedwater review where we can, and again at SAT."
    },
    {
     "q": "Can we keep our existing pretreatment when we replace the RO?",
     "a": "Often, if it meets the new RO inlet targets. We review its sizing, media age, sanitization capability and monitoring against your feed data, and tell you what to keep, upgrade or replace. See upgrades and retrofits."
    },
    {
     "q": "Our utility switches to free chlorine for a few weeks each year. Does that matter?",
     "a": "Yes, in both directions. Free chlorine reaches the carbon at a higher oxidant level, and the switch back changes what the bed must remove. Ask your utility for its schedule; we design for both disinfectants and alarm on total chlorine at the RO inlet."
    },
    {
     "q": "Who changes media, resin and cartridges after handover?",
